cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this note or its computations, please cite it as below.
  Please also cite the OpenAI preprints listed in UPSTREAM.md.
type: software
title: 'The leading constant of the memory-sample tradeoff for noiseless Gaussian regression'
version: 0.1.0
date-released: '2026-10-10'
authors:
  - name: '[AUTHOR NAME]'
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  OpenAI's preprint on subsphere methods shows that a streaming learner with
  o(d^2) bits of memory needs T >= 2^-16 d log2(1/eps) noiseless Gaussian
  samples to recover a unit signal within angle eps. The note shows that the
  constant 2^-16 can be replaced by any c < 2 ln 2, uniformly in
  0 < eps <= 1/10, and that randomized Kaczmarz with fixed-point registers
  matches this constant. In the window d(log2(1/eps) + 2 log2 d) <= M = o(d^2)
  the optimal sample count is therefore (2 ln 2 + o(1)) d log2(1/eps). The
  improvement (about 9.07e4) comes from a moving split point in the
  beta-mixture comparison, an exact beta-ratio bound and an arcsin terminal
  cap; with the original fixed split no parameter choice exceeds 0.5176. The
  statements are asymptotic in d and converge slowly; the sharp constant for
  Theta(d^2) memory is unknown. Conditional on the correctness of the upstream
  preprint. Found and checked by Claude (Anthropic) agents under the repository
  owner's direction. No human peer review has taken place and nothing is
  formalized in Lean.
keywords:
  - memory-sample tradeoffs
  - streaming lower bounds
  - linear regression
  - randomized Kaczmarz
  - explicit constants
